# Production server on Kubernetes

This journey builds on [Development server on Kubernetes](/self-hosting/kubernetes-development/).
It runs the hardened profile and a core tier of `all` replicas. Scale out by
adding `all` replicas first; when reads outgrow them, add an Oracle-only read
tier that a HorizontalPodAutoscaler grows on the reads its Oracles execute. The replicas find each other through
Postgres membership and talk over a private, mutually authenticated peer port.
The repository's local proof of this shape is `deploy/kubernetes/kind/`,
exercised by the `test:server:kind` journey; its values are local and are not
production defaults.

## Prerequisites

- A Kubernetes cluster with a CNI that enforces NetworkPolicy, a default
  StorageClass, Prometheus, and prometheus-adapter serving
  `custom.metrics.k8s.io` (or your provider's equivalent of both).
- A managed or operated PostgreSQL 16 with point-in-time backups, TLS with a
  certificate that names its host, the provider's CA certificate, and a login
  that owns the `wyrd` database.
- One object-store bucket (S3, GCS, or Azure Blob) and a cloud identity for the
  Wyrd pods that can read and write it.
- A service mesh that authenticates and encrypts the hop from your edge to
  the pods. This page uses [Istio](https://istio.io/) with sidecar injection
  and the Kubernetes Gateway API CRDs: the edge gateway terminates public TLS
  and reaches the pods over the mesh's mutual TLS. Another mesh works if it
  enforces the same policy. A load balancer that forwards plaintext to the
  pods does not: client tokens and bodies would cross the cluster network
  unprotected.
- Two public hostnames with one TLS certificate covering both: one for the
  API (this page uses `wyrd.example.com`) and one for read-only queries
  (`wyrd-query.example.com`).
- `openssl`, `psql` 15 or later, `kubectl`, a `bash` or `zsh` shell, and the `wyrd` CLI on an operator machine.

## The image

No Wyrd image is published yet. Until one is, production acceptance uses the
official image built from the reviewed commit and pinned by its image ID:

```bash
git rev-parse HEAD                     # the reviewed commit
mise run docker:build
docker image inspect -f '{{.Id}}' wyrd # record the ID with the commit
```

Push that image to your registry and reference it everywhere **by digest**
(`registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…`), never by a mutable tag. Once a release
is published, repeat acceptance against the published digest and deploy that.

## Six separate trust materials

Keep these apart. None of them substitutes for another.

| Material | Protects | Held by | Rotation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Public TLS certificate | Client traffic to your hostnames | Secret `wyrd-public-tls`, used only by the edge gateway | Your certificate manager |
| Mesh workload certificates | The hop from the edge gateway to the pods | The mesh (Istio issues and rotates them) | Automatic |
| Database logins (`<owner>`, `wyrd_app`, `wyrd_platform_admin`) and the database CA | Postgres, and proof that the pods reach the real server | Secrets `wyrd-owner` (migration Job only), `wyrd-db`, and `wyrd-db-ca` | `ALTER ROLE … PASSWORD`, update the Secret, roll the pods; replace `wyrd-db-ca` before your provider rotates its CA |
| Wyrd client credentials (`wyrd_global_…`, `wyrd_sk_…`) and the signing key | Wyrd API identity | Your secret manager; the signing key also in Secret `wyrd-signing` | See [Credentials](#credentials) |
| Object-store identity | The bucket | The provider's workload identity bound to the pods' ServiceAccount | Your cloud IAM |
| Peer certificates | The private peer port between Wyrd pods | Secret `wyrd-peer-tls` (CA key stays offline) | See [Peer TLS](#peer-tls) |

A peer certificate identifies a Wyrd process in this cluster. It does **not**
grant tenant identity: every receiver still checks the tenant, query, fence,
and deadline of each call against its own state, and public authentication is
unchanged.

## Environment

| Variable | When | Value | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `APP_ENV` | Required | `production` | Selects the hardened profile: a real signing key and gRPC reflection off. |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` in the migration Job | Required, Job only | owner URL ending `?sslmode=verify-full` | Secret `wyrd-owner`. |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` | Required | `wyrd_app` URL ending `?sslmode=verify-full` | Secret `wyrd-db`. |
| `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` | Required | `wyrd_platform_admin` URL ending `?sslmode=verify-full` | Secret `wyrd-db`. |
| `PGSSLROOTCERT` | Required, Job and pods | `/etc/wyrd/db-ca/ca.crt` | The standard Postgres variable: the CA every connection verifies the server's certificate against. Secret `wyrd-db-ca`. |
| `WYRD_STORAGE_URL` | Required | `s3://<bucket>`, `gs://<bucket>`, or `az://<container>` | One shared bucket. `file://` is process-local and cannot serve peers. |
| `AWS_REGION` or provider equivalent | Provider-dependent | your region | See [Storage](/self-hosting/storage/). Credentials come from the provider chain, bound to the pods' ServiceAccount (IRSA, GKE Workload Identity, Azure Workload Identity), not from static keys. |
| `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` | Required | `/run/wyrd/signing.pem` | Stable Ed25519 key, Secret `wyrd-signing`, shared by every replica. |
| `WYRD_BIFROST_DATA_DIR` | Set by the image | `/var/lib/wyrd/bifrost` | On each `all` replica, its own persistent volume: Scribe WAL, staging, and stable node identity. On an Oracle pod, ephemeral query scratch. |
| `WYRD_TARGET` | Required | `all` (core tier), `oracle` (read tier) | Which roles the pod runs. |
| `POD_IP` | Required | `status.podIP` | Downward API. |
| `WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS` | Required | `$(POD_IP):50052` | The address other replicas dial. No replica has an authored address. |
| `WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR` | Required | `/etc/wyrd/peer` | Secret `wyrd-peer-tls`: `ca.crt`, `tls.crt`, `tls.key`. |
| `WYRD_METRICS_BIND` | Required for autoscaling | `$(POD_IP):9464` | The Prometheus endpoint scraped for the Oracle read rate. |
| `WYRD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` | Conditional | `https://wyrd.example.com` | Only for human SSO. The same value goes in the web app's `ORIGIN`. See [SSO and OIDC](/self-hosting/sso-and-oidc/#deployment-setup). |
| `WYRD_UI_CLIENT_SECRET_SHA256` | Conditional | from Secret `wyrd-ui-client` | Only for web-app SSO. The web app reads the secret itself as `WYRD_UI_CLIENT_SECRET`. |
| `WYRD_SEALING_KEY_FILE` | Conditional | `/run/wyrd-sealing/key` | Only if a stored OIDC connection or issuer carries a client secret. |
| `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` | Conditional | e.g. `acme` | Only once you seed a workload issuer or binding at boot. The tenant must exist first. See [SSO and OIDC](/self-hosting/sso-and-oidc/). |

## 1. Database roles and Secrets

Every database URL ends in `?sslmode=verify-full`. With it, each connection
requires TLS, checks that the server's certificate chains to your provider's
CA, and checks that it names the host in the URL. Without it, a connection can
reach an impostor or run unencrypted. Get the CA certificate from your
provider (for example, the RDS or Cloud SQL server CA bundle) and save it as
`db-ca.crt`:

Load the three passwords into shell variables from your secret manager (for
example `read -rs OWNER_PW`), using long random URL-safe values such as
`openssl rand -hex 32`. They never go on a command line: `psql` reads a
password from `PGPASSWORD`, and `kubectl` reads each URL from a pipe.

```bash
DB="<db-host>:5432/wyrd?sslmode=verify-full"
export PGSSLROOTCERT=$PWD/db-ca.crt
```

Bootstrap the serving roles once as the owner, exactly as in the
[development guide](/self-hosting/kubernetes-development/)
but with `"postgresql://<owner>@$DB"`. Then check all three logins. Each line
must print the role and `t` (encrypted):

```bash
Q='SELECT current_user, ssl FROM pg_stat_ssl WHERE pid = pg_backend_pid()'
PGPASSWORD="$OWNER_PW" psql "postgresql://<owner>@$DB" -Atc "$Q"
PGPASSWORD="$APP_PW" psql "postgresql://wyrd_app@$DB" -Atc "$Q"
PGPASSWORD="$PLATFORM_PW" psql "postgresql://wyrd_platform_admin@$DB" -Atc "$Q"
```

If the server's certificate does not chain to `db-ca.crt` or does not name
`<db-host>`, `psql` fails here with a certificate error, and the pods and
the migration Job would refuse the same connection. Fix the host name or the
CA file; never lower `sslmode`.

Create the Secrets from your secret manager (External Secrets, CSI secret
store, or `kubectl create secret` from a trusted machine), never from files
committed anywhere:

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-owner \
  --from-file=database-url=<(printf %s "postgresql://<owner>:$OWNER_PW@$DB")
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-db \
  --from-file=database-url=<(printf %s "postgresql://wyrd_app:$APP_PW@$DB") \
  --from-file=platform-database-url=<(printf %s "postgresql://wyrd_platform_admin:$PLATFORM_PW@$DB")
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-db-ca --from-file=ca.crt=db-ca.crt
kubectl -n wyrd create secret tls wyrd-public-tls --cert=public.crt --key=public.key
openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out signing.pem
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-signing --from-file=signing.pem
# store signing.pem in your secret manager, then delete the local copy
```

## Peer TLS

Create one **dedicated** CA for this cluster's peers and one leaf shared by
every replica, with DNS name `wyrd-peer`. Do this on a trusted machine. The CA
private key never enters the cluster.

```bash
openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes -days 365 \
  -subj "/CN=Wyrd peer CA" -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt \
  -addext basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE -addext keyUsage=critical,keyCertSign,cRLSign
openssl req -new -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes -subj "/CN=wyrd-peer" \
  -keyout tls.key -out tls.csr
openssl x509 -req -in tls.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -days 90 -out tls.crt \
  -extfile <(printf '%s\n' 'subjectAltName=DNS:wyrd-peer' \
    'keyUsage=critical,digitalSignature' 'extendedKeyUsage=serverAuth,clientAuth')
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-peer-tls \
  --from-file=ca.crt --from-file=tls.crt --from-file=tls.key
# move ca.key to offline storage; delete tls.key and tls.csr locally
```

Use a CA made only for this purpose: every certificate it signs is a peer.
To rotate the leaf, sign a new one from the same CA, replace the Secret, and
roll the core StatefulSet and the Oracle Deployment. If the leaf key is exposed, do the same immediately; if
the CA key is exposed, create a new CA and leaf and roll every pod.

## 2. Back up, then migrate

Every migration, first install or upgrade, follows the same order:

1. Take a database backup (or confirm a point-in-time recovery point) and
   **verify it restores**. Record the recovery point with the image digest.
2. Run the migration Job below with the new image. It is the only workload
   that receives the owner URL. It is the
   [development one](/self-hosting/kubernetes-development/)
   plus the database CA.
3. On success, roll the core StatefulSet and the Oracle Deployment to the
   same digest.

```yaml
# migrate.yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata: {name: wyrd-migrate, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  backoffLimit: 0
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…
          command: [wyrd-server, migrate]
          env:
            - name: WYRD_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-owner, key: database-url}}
            - {name: PGSSLROOTCERT, value: /etc/wyrd/db-ca/ca.crt}
          volumeMounts:
            - {name: db-ca, mountPath: /etc/wyrd/db-ca, readOnly: true}
      volumes:
        - name: db-ca
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-db-ca}
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f migrate.yaml
kubectl -n wyrd wait --for=condition=complete job/wyrd-migrate --timeout=300s
kubectl -n wyrd logs job/wyrd-migrate    # -> Wyrd and Vala schemas are migrated and valid.
kubectl -n wyrd delete job wyrd-migrate
```

If the Job fails, **stop**. Do not roll the serving pods: pods of the old image
keep serving only if the schema still matches them, and every pod refuses a
schema that does not match its binary with `WYRD_SQL_503_SCHEMA_NOT_READY`
rather than serving it. Read the Job log, repair the cause (connectivity, a
certificate error, a lock held by another migrator, a failed statement), and rerun the Job; a
completed `migrate` is idempotent. If the database cannot be repaired, restore
the verified backup, then rerun the Job.

## 3. Serve: scale `all` replicas, then add an Oracle tier

`wyrd-core` is a StatefulSet of `all` replicas. Each one runs a Scribe, a Forge
worker, a Forge coordinator, and an Oracle, and serves the whole public API,
including Bifrost ingest. Scale out by adding `all` replicas first: every
replica takes ingest and reads, and each Oracle fuses the live rows of every
replica's Scribe over the peer port. Forge planning is a singleton held by a
lease, so one coordinator plans while the others stand by, ready, and take over
when its lease lapses; every replica's worker executes the planned compaction.

Move to role-specific pods when one workload outgrows the shared shape. The
usual first step is `wyrd-oracle`: pods with `WYRD_TARGET=oracle` that serve
public reads and execute each read on their own Oracle. They fetch live, not
yet published rows from the core replicas' Scribes over the peer port and
published data from the shared bucket. They own no Scribe WAL or node identity,
so they are a Deployment on ephemeral scratch, autoscaled on their read rate.
With no Scribe they refuse Bifrost ingest: send ingest to the core tier. Skip
steps 4 and 6 below until you add this tier.

Apply the manifests in this order, each into namespace `wyrd`:

1. `wyrd-netpol.yaml` and `wyrd-mesh.yaml`: the network and mesh policies in
   [Restrict who reaches the pods](#restrict-who-reaches-the-pods), before any
   serving pod exists.
2. `wyrd-sa.yaml`: the ServiceAccount below.
3. `wyrd-core.yaml`: the core Service and StatefulSet, then wait for it.
4. `wyrd-oracle.yaml`: the Oracle Service and Deployment, then wait for it.
5. `wyrd-edge.yaml`: the edge gateway and routes in [Public routing](#public-routing).
6. The scrape job and adapter rule, then `wyrd-oracle-hpa.yaml`, in [Autoscaling](#4-autoscaling).

Both workloads carry the mesh sidecar. Their public ports `8080` and `50051`
go through it, so only mesh-authenticated callers reach them. The peer port
`50052` and the metrics port `9464` bypass it: peers already use their own
mutual TLS, and Prometheus scrapes from outside the mesh.

```yaml
# wyrd-sa.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: wyrd
  namespace: wyrd
  # Bind the object-store identity here, for example:
  # annotations: {eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::…:role/wyrd}
```

```yaml
# wyrd-core.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: {name: wyrd, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  selector: {app: wyrd-core}
  ports:
    - {name: http, port: 8080}
    - {name: grpc, port: 50051}
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata: {name: wyrd-core, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  serviceName: wyrd
  replicas: 2                         # add `all` replicas to scale out
  selector: {matchLabels: {app: wyrd-core}}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: {app: wyrd-core, app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd, sidecar.istio.io/inject: "true"}
      annotations:
        wyrd.ai/scrape-port: "9464"
        traffic.sidecar.istio.io/excludeInboundPorts: "50052,9464"
        traffic.sidecar.istio.io/excludeOutboundPorts: "50052"
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: wyrd
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45
      containers:
        - name: wyrd
          image: registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…
          env:
            - {name: APP_ENV, value: production}
            - {name: WYRD_TARGET, value: all}
            - name: POD_IP
              valueFrom: {fieldRef: {fieldPath: status.podIP}}
            - {name: WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS, value: "$(POD_IP):50052"}
            - {name: WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR, value: /etc/wyrd/peer}
            - {name: WYRD_METRICS_BIND, value: "$(POD_IP):9464"}
            - name: WYRD_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: database-url}}
            - name: WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: platform-database-url}}
            - {name: PGSSLROOTCERT, value: /etc/wyrd/db-ca/ca.crt}
            - {name: WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE, value: /run/wyrd/signing.pem}
            - {name: WYRD_STORAGE_URL, value: "s3://your-bucket"}
            - {name: AWS_REGION, value: us-east-1}
          ports:
            - {name: http, containerPort: 8080}
            - {name: grpc, containerPort: 50051}
            - {name: peer, containerPort: 50052}
            - {name: metrics, containerPort: 9464}
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /readyz, port: http}
            periodSeconds: 2
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: http}
            periodSeconds: 10
          # See "Sizing" below: size from load tests, never below 3.75 GiB.
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "2", memory: 8Gi}
            limits: {cpu: "4", memory: 8Gi}
          volumeMounts:
            - {name: data, mountPath: /var/lib/wyrd}
            - {name: peer-tls, mountPath: /etc/wyrd/peer, readOnly: true}
            - {name: signing, mountPath: /run/wyrd, readOnly: true}
            - {name: db-ca, mountPath: /etc/wyrd/db-ca, readOnly: true}
      volumes:
        - name: peer-tls
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-peer-tls}
        - name: signing
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-signing}
        - name: db-ca
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-db-ca}
  volumeClaimTemplates:
    - metadata: {name: data}
      spec: {accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce], resources: {requests: {storage: 50Gi}}}
```

Each core replica's volume holds its Scribe WAL and node identity. Never
replace it with `emptyDir`: a lost Scribe volume is node loss, not an empty
restart.

```yaml
# wyrd-oracle.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: {name: wyrd-oracle, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  selector: {app: wyrd-oracle}
  ports:
    - {name: http, port: 8080}
    - {name: grpc, port: 50051}
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata: {name: wyrd-oracle, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  replicas: 2                         # the HPA owns this once applied
  selector: {matchLabels: {app: wyrd-oracle}}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: {app: wyrd-oracle, app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd, sidecar.istio.io/inject: "true"}
      annotations:
        wyrd.ai/scrape-port: "9464"
        traffic.sidecar.istio.io/excludeInboundPorts: "50052,9464"
        traffic.sidecar.istio.io/excludeOutboundPorts: "50052"
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: wyrd
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45
      containers:
        - name: wyrd
          image: registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…   # the core tier's digest
          env:
            - {name: APP_ENV, value: production}
            - {name: WYRD_TARGET, value: oracle}
            - name: POD_IP
              valueFrom: {fieldRef: {fieldPath: status.podIP}}
            - {name: WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS, value: "$(POD_IP):50052"}
            - {name: WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR, value: /etc/wyrd/peer}
            - {name: WYRD_METRICS_BIND, value: "$(POD_IP):9464"}
            - name: WYRD_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: database-url}}
            - name: WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: platform-database-url}}
            - {name: PGSSLROOTCERT, value: /etc/wyrd/db-ca/ca.crt}
            - {name: WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE, value: /run/wyrd/signing.pem}
            - {name: WYRD_STORAGE_URL, value: "s3://your-bucket"}
            - {name: AWS_REGION, value: us-east-1}
          ports:
            - {name: http, containerPort: 8080}
            - {name: grpc, containerPort: 50051}
            - {name: peer, containerPort: 50052}
            - {name: metrics, containerPort: 9464}
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /readyz, port: http}
            periodSeconds: 2
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: http}
            periodSeconds: 10
          resources:                  # from load tests; see "Sizing"
            requests: {cpu: "2", memory: 4Gi}
            limits: {cpu: "4", memory: 4Gi}
          volumeMounts:
            - {name: scratch, mountPath: /var/lib/wyrd}
            - {name: peer-tls, mountPath: /etc/wyrd/peer, readOnly: true}
            - {name: signing, mountPath: /run/wyrd, readOnly: true}
            - {name: db-ca, mountPath: /etc/wyrd/db-ca, readOnly: true}
      volumes:
        - name: scratch               # query spill; lost on restart by design
          emptyDir: {sizeLimit: 20Gi}
        - name: peer-tls
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-peer-tls}
        - name: signing
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-signing}
        - name: db-ca
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-db-ca}
```

The Oracle pods use the same serving logins, database CA, signing key, bucket
identity, and peer bundle as the core tier. Write `wyrd-netpol.yaml` and
`wyrd-mesh.yaml` from [Restrict who reaches the pods](#restrict-who-reaches-the-pods)
and `wyrd-edge.yaml` from [Public routing](#public-routing) first. Apply the
policies before any serving pod exists, so no pod ever listens unprotected,
then the workloads, then the edge:

```bash
kubectl apply -f wyrd-netpol.yaml -f wyrd-mesh.yaml
kubectl apply -f wyrd-sa.yaml -f wyrd-core.yaml
kubectl -n wyrd rollout status statefulset/wyrd-core --timeout=600s
kubectl apply -f wyrd-oracle.yaml
kubectl -n wyrd rollout status deployment/wyrd-oracle --timeout=600s
kubectl apply -f wyrd-edge.yaml
```

A replica enters ready membership only once its peer listener is serving. One
that cannot bind or serve its peer port stays out of the ready set, so no other
replica routes work to it.

### Restrict who reaches the pods

Two layers apply, and both are required. The NetworkPolicy admits the public
ports `8080`/`50051` only from the edge gateway's pods, the peer port `50052`
only from pods labelled `app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd`, and scrapes on `9464`
only from the `monitoring` namespace where Prometheus runs:

```yaml
# wyrd-netpol.yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata: {name: wyrd, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  podSelector: {matchLabels: {app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd}}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
    - from: [{podSelector: {matchLabels: {gateway.networking.k8s.io/gateway-name: wyrd-edge}}}]
      ports: [{port: 8080}, {port: 50051}]
    - from: [{podSelector: {matchLabels: {app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd}}}]
      ports: [{port: 50052}]
    - from: [{namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring}}}]
      ports: [{port: 9464}]
```

The mesh policy makes the gateway-to-pod hop mutual TLS only and accepts it
only from the edge gateway's mesh identity. A plaintext connection, or one
from any other workload, is refused even if the network allows it:

```yaml
# wyrd-mesh.yaml
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata: {name: wyrd, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  selector: {matchLabels: {app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd}}
  mtls: {mode: STRICT}
---
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata: {name: wyrd-public, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  selector: {matchLabels: {app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd}}
  action: ALLOW
  rules:
    - from: [{source: {principals: [cluster.local/ns/wyrd/sa/wyrd-edge-istio]}}]
      to: [{operation: {ports: ["8080", "50051"]}}]
```

Check both from an ordinary pod. Each request must fail, not print `200`:

```bash
POD_IP=$(kubectl -n wyrd get pod wyrd-core-0 -o jsonpath='{.status.podIP}')
kubectl -n wyrd run probe --rm -i --restart=Never --image=curlimages/curl -- \
  curl -sS -m 5 -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' "http://$POD_IP:8080/readyz"
```

### Public routing

The edge gateway terminates public TLS with Secret `wyrd-public-tls` and is
the only public entry. Clients derive the gRPC address from the server URL
(same host, port `50051`), so each hostname listens on `443` for HTTP (API and
UI) and `50051` for gRPC. `wyrd.example.com` routes to Service `wyrd` (the
core tier); `wyrd-query.example.com` routes to Service `wyrd-oracle`. Clients
that only query set `WYRD_SERVER_URL=https://wyrd-query.example.com`;
everything else, including every Bifrost writer, uses `wyrd.example.com`.
Nothing routes to `50052`.

The gRPC listener uses `HTTPRoute`s too: gRPC is HTTP/2, and the backend port
name `grpc` makes the mesh speak HTTP/2 upstream. Do not use `GRPCRoute` here.
Istio drops a `GRPCRoute` whose hostname matches an `HTTPRoute` on another
listener, and gRPC then gets `UNIMPLEMENTED` from the gateway.

```yaml
# wyrd-edge.yaml
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Gateway
metadata: {name: wyrd-edge, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  gatewayClassName: istio
  listeners:
    - name: https
      port: 443
      protocol: HTTPS
      tls: {mode: Terminate, certificateRefs: [{name: wyrd-public-tls}]}
    - name: grpc
      port: 50051
      protocol: HTTPS
      tls: {mode: Terminate, certificateRefs: [{name: wyrd-public-tls}]}
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata: {name: wyrd, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  parentRefs: [{name: wyrd-edge, sectionName: https}]
  hostnames: [wyrd.example.com]
  rules: [{backendRefs: [{name: wyrd, port: 8080}]}]
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata: {name: wyrd-grpc, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  parentRefs: [{name: wyrd-edge, sectionName: grpc}]
  hostnames: [wyrd.example.com]
  rules: [{backendRefs: [{name: wyrd, port: 50051}]}]
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata: {name: wyrd-query, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  parentRefs: [{name: wyrd-edge, sectionName: https}]
  hostnames: [wyrd-query.example.com]
  rules: [{backendRefs: [{name: wyrd-oracle, port: 8080}]}]
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata: {name: wyrd-query-grpc, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  parentRefs: [{name: wyrd-edge, sectionName: grpc}]
  hostnames: [wyrd-query.example.com]
  rules: [{backendRefs: [{name: wyrd-oracle, port: 50051}]}]
```

Istio runs the gateway as Deployment `wyrd-edge-istio` with mesh identity
`cluster.local/ns/wyrd/sa/wyrd-edge-istio`, the principal the mesh policy
admits, behind a `LoadBalancer` Service of the same name. Point both
hostnames' DNS at that Service's external address:

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd wait --for=condition=programmed gateway/wyrd-edge --timeout=300s
kubectl -n wyrd get gateway wyrd-edge    # ADDRESS is the public entry
```

## 4. Autoscaling

The Oracle tier scales on **successful reads its Oracles execute per second**.
Each Oracle counts its completed reads in the histogram
`oracle_query_duration_seconds`; its `_count` series with
`outcome="success"` is that count. A read that ends `degraded` (for example,
with a live tail unavailable) has its own `outcome="degraded"` series and is
not counted as success. HTTP request counts and CPU are not the signal: they
do not measure Oracle execution.

**Scrape each pod with its namespace and pod labels.** The adapter attributes
a series to a pod only through those two labels. Add this job to your
Prometheus configuration (with the Prometheus Operator, a `PodMonitor` on
`app.kubernetes.io/name: wyrd` with the same relabelings does the same). Its
ServiceAccount needs `get`, `list`, and `watch` on pods in namespace `wyrd`.

```yaml
scrape_configs:
  - job_name: wyrd
    kubernetes_sd_configs:
      - role: pod
        namespaces: {names: [wyrd]}
    relabel_configs:
      - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_annotation_wyrd_ai_scrape_port]
        regex: .+
        action: keep
      - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_ip, __meta_kubernetes_pod_annotation_wyrd_ai_scrape_port]
        regex: (.+);(.+)
        replacement: $1:$2
        target_label: __address__
      - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_namespace]
        target_label: namespace
      - source_labels: [__meta_kubernetes_pod_name]
        target_label: pod
```

**Expose the per-pod rate.** Add one prometheus-adapter rule:

```yaml
rules:
  - seriesQuery: 'oracle_query_duration_seconds_count{namespace!="",pod!="",outcome="success"}'
    resources: {overrides: {namespace: {resource: namespace}, pod: {resource: pod}}}
    name: {matches: ^oracle_query_duration_seconds_count$, as: wyrd_oracle_reads_per_second}
    metricsQuery: 'sum(rate(<<.Series>>{<<.LabelMatchers>>,outcome="success"}[1m])) by (<<.GroupBy>>)'
```

Before applying the autoscaler, confirm the adapter serves a value for every
Oracle pod:

```bash
kubectl get --raw "/apis/custom.metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/namespaces/wyrd/pods/*/wyrd_oracle_reads_per_second?labelSelector=app%3Dwyrd-oracle"
```

Each item names one `wyrd-oracle-…` pod and its current rate. An empty list or
a `NotFound` error means the scrape job or relabels are not in place yet; the
autoscaler would report `FailedGetPodsMetric` and never scale.

**Apply the autoscaler:**

```yaml
# wyrd-oracle-hpa.yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata: {name: wyrd-oracle, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  scaleTargetRef: {apiVersion: apps/v1, kind: Deployment, name: wyrd-oracle}
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 8                      # cap: from load tests and database capacity
  metrics:
    - type: Pods
      pods:
        metric: {name: wyrd_oracle_reads_per_second}
        target: {type: AverageValue, averageValue: "<from load testing>"}
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f wyrd-oracle-hpa.yaml
kubectl -n wyrd get hpa wyrd-oracle    # TARGETS shows a measured value, not <unknown>
```

The target and cap are yours to measure. Load-test the reads your tenants
actually issue against your database and object store, and set the target
below the per-pod Oracle-executed read rate where latency or errors degrade.
Do **not** copy the kind journey's 10 reads per second: it is a small local
number chosen so a test scales quickly. Size `maxReplicas` so every replica's
Postgres connections fit the database.

After a scale-up, prove the join: the new Oracle pod is Ready, it is listed in
ready membership as `oracle` at its own pod IP, and a strict fused read sent
through it returns rows a core replica's Scribe just acknowledged while the
core replicas' own Oracles execute nothing. The kind journey checks exactly this.

## Sizing

Scribe, Oracle, Forge, and in-flight ingest share one Bifrost memory cap. By
default it is the pod's memory limit less a 1 GiB minimum kept for other
`wyrd-server` work, so an 8 GiB pod gives Bifrost 7 GiB. Raise that minimum with
`WYRD_SERVER_MEMORY_MIN_BYTES`, or lower the cap with
`WYRD_BIFROST_MEMORY_LIMIT_BYTES`. A pod that cannot keep the minimum and still
give Bifrost memory refuses to boot. No role reserves a fixed share, so an idle
role costs nothing.

The local kind core replica runs at 4 GiB, a 3 GiB cap: enough for the journey, not
for production queries or compaction. Set requests equal to limits from load
tests, and give the Oracle tier its own measured size and scratch. A query that
exhausts its memory fails alone with `WYRD_VALA_503_QUERY_RESOURCES_EXHAUSTED`,
and SDKs do not retry it automatically.

## 5. First-use setup and credentials

Run setup once, through `exec`, so credentials print to your terminal and not
to any log:

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd exec wyrd-core-0 -- wyrd-server setup --tenant acme
```

It prints the platform root credential (`wyrd_global_…`) and the tenant
`admin_credential: wyrd_sk_…`, each exactly once. Put both in your secret
manager immediately. Then issue narrower credentials for workloads instead of
sharing the admin one (`wyrd principal credential create-principal`), and API
keys bound to a Card with `wyrd auth issue-key` where that fits.

### Credentials

Rotate every Wyrd credential with an overlap: issue the replacement, deploy and
verify it, then revoke the old one.

```bash
export WYRD_SERVER_URL=https://wyrd.example.com
# Tenant and workload credentials (acting with a tenant admin credential):
wyrd principal credential issue  --principal <principal-id>
wyrd principal credential revoke --principal <principal-id> --credential-id <old-id>
# Platform credentials (reads WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL):
wyrd platform credential issue  --principal <platform-principal-id>
wyrd platform credential revoke --principal <platform-principal-id> --credential-id <old-id>
```

If every platform credential is lost, `kubectl exec wyrd-core-0 -- wyrd-server recover-root`
reissues one using the pod's database access. A lost tenant admin credential is
recovered with `wyrd platform tenant recover-admin`. The full matrix is in
[Administering the deployment](/self-hosting/running-the-server/#administering-the-deployment).
Rotating the signing key invalidates issued access tokens; plan it as described
in [Authentication](/self-hosting/authentication/).

### OIDC

Human single sign-on adds `WYRD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` and `WYRD_UI_CLIENT_SECRET_SHA256`
on every pod, with `ORIGIN` and `WYRD_UI_CLIENT_SECRET` for the web app, and
`WYRD_SEALING_KEY_FILE` (a base64 32-byte key in its own Secret) if a
connection stores a client secret. Each tenant then stages and activates its
own connection at runtime. Rate-limit `POST /auth/device` per client address at
your ingress. Seeding workload issuers at boot also needs
`WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG=acme`. See [SSO and OIDC](/self-hosting/sso-and-oidc/).

## 6. Verify

```bash
curl -s https://wyrd.example.com/readyz       # 200
export WYRD_API_KEY=wyrd_sk_…
wyrd apply hello && wyrd list --kind Prompt
```

Then restart a core replica and the Oracle tier, wait for both, and list again:
the Card, API keys, issued tokens, and the core replica's volume survive.

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd delete pod wyrd-core-0
kubectl -n wyrd rollout restart deployment/wyrd-oracle
kubectl -n wyrd rollout status statefulset/wyrd-core
kubectl -n wyrd rollout status deployment/wyrd-oracle
wyrd list --kind Prompt
```

## Failure and recovery

| Symptom | Cause | Recovery |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pods exit with `WYRD_SQL_503_SCHEMA_NOT_READY` | Schema and image disagree, or grants or policies drifted. | Run the migration Job for the deployed digest. Never hand-edit the schema. |
| Migration Job fails | See step 2. | Stop, repair, rerun; restore the verified backup if the database cannot be repaired. |
| A pod refuses to boot in production | Missing signing key, gRPC reflection on, or a missing required input. | The log names the input. Fix the Secret or env. |
| A new Oracle never turns Ready | Its peer port cannot bind or serve, or it cannot reach Postgres. | Check the pod log, the peer Secret, and the NetworkPolicy. It stays out of routing until fixed. |
| The HPA shows `<unknown>` or `FailedGetPodsMetric` | Prometheus does not scrape the pods with `namespace` and `pod` labels, or the adapter rule is missing. | Fix the scrape job and rule, then rerun the custom-metrics check above. |
| Bifrost ingest to the query hostname fails | Oracle pods have no Scribe to acknowledge rows. | Send ingest to `wyrd.example.com`. |
| Peer calls fail with TLS errors | A pod mounts a leaf from a different CA, or the leaf expired. | Reissue from the cluster CA, replace `wyrd-peer-tls`, roll the core StatefulSet and the Oracle Deployment. |
| Storage errors | The ServiceAccount lacks the bucket identity. | Fix the workload identity binding; do not add static keys. |
| A core replica's volume is lost | Scribe node loss, not an empty restart. | Follow the Scribe node or volume loss runbook before readmitting it. |

